{
  "requirement": "In a design session with no code to write, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop after the requirements commit.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation: the `habeas-requirements` skill must tell agents to stop after the requirements commit when a design session has no code to write.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_after_the_commit_in_a_design_session]` checks this directly:\n- It locates the workflow step that contains \"all groups together\". That is the commit step, step 5 in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. It finds the step with `step_of`, so the test does not hard-code the step number.\n- It extracts that step with `step`, which uses `workflow_steps`. That helper joins the step's indented continuation lines and collapses their whitespace. The wrapped sentence \"In a design session / with no code to write, stop here.\" therefore matches as one phrase.\n- It calls `assert_text_in_order` to require \"then commit it\" and then \"In a design session with no code to write, stop here\" after it, within that step. A skill that dropped the sentence, or moved it to another step, would fail the test. So would one that placed it before the commit instruction.\n\nThe skill text matches the assertions: the commit step says to commit, then says \"In a design session with no code to write, stop here.\"\n\nThe requirement names no other cases or boundaries, so I found no other scenarios that need tests.",
